==============================================================================================================
VERDICT
==============================================================================================================
  1. KMOS3D DOES NOT REPLICATE RC100's SEPARATION.  The pre-declared test failed: every model's residuals fall with z, the
     framework's at -0.000..+0.013 dex/z (1.7 sigma).  The cause is common-mode: Newtonian baryons alone fall at
     -0.03 dex/z or steeper, and at z ~ 2.3 0%-5% of galaxies (by geometry) rotate slower than their own baryons (median gas/star
     0.46 -> 1.19, from scaling relations).  No model that adds gravity fits those; the models' own trends differ by <=
     0.031 dex/z.  KMOS3D's v_circ x scaling-relation baryons cannot decide framework vs LCDM.
  2. L323's TREND RESULT IS DOWNGRADED TO CONDITIONAL.  It survives every halo knob but flips inside a baryon-calibration tilt of
     ~0.1 dex/z (LCDM within 2 sigma from beta = -0.05; framework 2 sigma off from beta = -0.1), and one galaxy at the
     f_DM = 0.02 edge moves it 0.40 sigma.  KMOS3D shows the high-z inputs can carry a z-dependent baryon error; its sign
     (baryons over-estimated at high z) is the one that moves RC100's data TOWARD LCDM, if RC100's fitted masses share it.
  3. What would decide it: high-z dark fractions from kinematics with DIRECT gas masses (CO/dust per galaxy), not scaling
     relations -- the input, not the halo, is now the lever.

  1/5 checks pass; load-bearing failures: 2; wrote L332_kmos3d_trend_replication_results_MUTATE.json
